<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banu Hud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1039–<a href="/years/1110">1110</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Hudids, Hudid dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hudid dynasty (Banu Hud) ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> from 1039 to 1110. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulayman-ibn-hud">Sulayman ibn Hud al-Mustain</a>, it reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-muqtadir">Ahmad al-Muqtadir</a>, who annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aljaferia">Aljafería</a> palace, and it employed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> in the 1080s. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> took Zaragoza in 1110 the family held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rueda-de-jalon">Rueda de Jalón</a>, and a descendant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hud">Ibn Hud</a>, led a revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in the 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banu-hud.jpg" alt="Banu Hud" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ecelan · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Aljafer%C3%ADa_-_Palacio_taifa_02.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Almoravids took Zaragoza in 1110, the <b>Hudid</b> heir Abd al-Malik Imad al-Dawla withdrew to the strong castle of Rueda de Jalón, west of the city, and continued to rule a small territory from there, relying on Alfonso I of Aragon against the Almoravids.</blockquote><a href="/state/rueda-de-jalon-hudid">Rueda de Jalón (Hudid)</a></li><li><blockquote>He briefly claimed recognition from other cities, but was driven out for a time by the <b>Hudid</b> prince Zafadola and had to seek help from Alfonso VII of Castile, while the Almoravid commander Yahya ibn Ghaniya fought to retake the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-taifa-of-cordoba">Second Taifa of Córdoba</a></li><li><blockquote>Its governor Sulayman ibn Hud ruled Lleida for years before he took Zaragoza in 1039 and founded the <b>Hudid dynasty</b>; on his death in 1046 his son Yusuf al-Muzaffar ruled it as an independent kingdom, fighting his brother al-Muqtadir of the Taifa of Zaragoza.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-lerida">Taifa of Lérida</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1060 the <b>Hudid</b> ruler al-Muqtadir of Zaragoza annexed Tortosa, ending its independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-tortosa">Taifa of Tortosa</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1039 the Tujibids were overthrown and Sulayman ibn Hud al-Mustain founded the <b>Hudid dynasty</b>, which went on to rule for some seventy years.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-zaragoza">Taifa of Zaragoza</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1228 Muhammad ibn Yusuf ibn Hud, claiming descent from the <b>Hudids</b> of Zaragoza, rose against the Almohads in the Ricote valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/third-taifa-of-murcia">Third Taifa of Murcia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-house"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Rulers and members</h2><figure class="pp-chart"><p class="cty-scale" aria-hidden="true"><span>1075</span><span>1175</span><span>1275</span></p><ol class="cty-tl pp-tl"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-muqtadir"><span class="pp-av"></span><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Ahmad al-Muqtadir</span><span class="cty-y">d. 1081</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:3.0%;width:0.8%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-al-mutaman-ibn-hud"><span class="pp-av"></span><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Yusuf al-Mutaman ibn Hud</span><span class="cty-y">d. 1085</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:5.0%;width:0.8%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-imad-al-dawla"><span class="pp-av"></span><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Abd al-Malik Imad al-Dawla</span><span class="cty-y">d. 1130</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:27.5%;width:0.8%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/zafadola"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zafadola.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Zafadola</span><span class="cty-y">d. 1146</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:35.5%;width:0.8%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hud"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ibn-hud.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Ibn Hud</span><span class="cty-y">d. 1238</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:81.5%;width:0.8%"></span></span></li></ol><figcaption>5 people of the encyclopedia whose Wikidata record names this house as their family.